-- THE UNRELATED MODULE.  It takes no part in the collision it is surrounded by: it
-- declares its OWN `Dup`, at its OWN type, and the binding asserted about (`bys`)
-- has type `Int` — it mentions neither `CA` nor `CB` nor anything either colliding
-- module declares.
--
-- It nonetheless imports colla's constructors WITH `(..)`, which is what makes this
-- able to fail rather than green by construction: colla's `Dup` is therefore in
-- scope AND witnessed by an import of this module, so a scope decision that omitted
-- the own-declaration rung would find exactly ONE witnessed candidate and override
-- this module's `Dup` to colla's `Int -> CA`.  `unPlain (Dup 5)` then does not
-- typecheck.  (That is the A-2.6 layering regression — `import json.{Json, JNull}`
-- clobbering a local `data Tok = JString String` — one namespace over.)
--
-- ⚠️ MEASURED, not reasoned: with `scopedCtorEntry`'s own-declaration rung stubbed
-- out and nothing else changed, on a binary built from this branch, `entry.mdk`
-- goes from `7`/`b`/`25` at exit 0 to exit 1 with `Type mismatch: CA vs Plain` at
-- `unPlain (Dup n) = n` (bystand.mdk:24) plus two more.  This cell can fail.
--
-- The rest of the colla import must keep working at the same time: `ACount` is not
-- collided anywhere and is used here, so "the local ctor wins" cannot be bought by
-- dropping the import.
import colla.{CA(..), unCA}

public export data Plain =
  | Dup Int
  | Skip

unPlain : Plain -> Int
unPlain (Dup n) = n
unPlain Skip = 0

export bys : Int
bys = unPlain (Dup 5) + unCA (ACount 2)
